---
import ProjectLayout from '../../layouts/ProjectLayout.astro';
import FigureRow from '../../components/portfolio/FigureRow.astro';
import PortfolioSection from '../../components/portfolio/PortfolioSection.astro';
import PortfolioSectionHeader from '../../components/portfolio/PortfolioSectionHeader.astro';
import ProjectFigure from '../../components/portfolio/ProjectFigure.astro';
import ProjectHero from '../../components/portfolio/ProjectHero.astro';
import ResponsiveImage from '../../components/ResponsiveImage.astro';
---

<ProjectLayout
  title="Rooftop Garden"
  subtitle="A typhoon tore the iron roof off Weixin Hall—the fallen roof became a canvas for the people of the building."
  description="An interactive projection proposal that keeps a storm's leftover as a living part of the building."
  period="2024"
  kind="Interactive Installation Concept · Digital Media Art"
  status="Course proposal"
  projectClass="portfolio-page--roof"
  nextHref="/work/robotaxi-cabin"
  nextTitle="The Empathetic Robotaxi"
>
  <ProjectHero class="project-hero--roof">
    <ResponsiveImage
      src="/pictures/work/rooftop-garden/rg-hero.png"
      alt="The fallen iron roof in the atrium of Weixin Hall"
      priority
    />
    <p>
      The roof stayed with the building for fourteen years. Then a storm moved it into the atrium.
    </p>
  </ProjectHero>

  <PortfolioSection>
    <PortfolioSectionHeader title="The Storm Left Something Behind" />
    <p class="portfolio-copy portfolio-copy--flush">
      During the 2024 Mid-Autumn holiday, typhoon Bebinca made landfall in Shanghai—the strongest
      typhoon to hit the city since 1949. It tore the iron roof of the car photography studio off
      Weixin Hall and dropped it into the atrium.
    </p>
    <p class="portfolio-copy portfolio-copy--flush">
      The fallen roof broke the monotony of the atrium and became a new talking point for people
      passing by. Compared with clearing the remnant away, this project asks what it could become
      instead.
    </p>
    <ProjectFigure class="project-figure--white">
      <ResponsiveImage
        src="/pictures/work/rooftop-garden/rg-p3-1.png"
        alt="Weixin Hall, School of Art and Communication, Tongji University"
      />
      <figcaption>Weixin Hall · background photo by Hengzhong Lv.</figcaption>
    </ProjectFigure>
  </PortfolioSection>

  <PortfolioSection class="portfolio-section--dark">
    <div class="portfolio-section__inner">
      <PortfolioSectionHeader title="Fourteen Years of Memory" />
      <p class="portfolio-copy portfolio-copy--flush">
        The iron roof had silently accompanied the students of Weixin Hall for fourteen years.
        Instead of paying the high cost of removal, the proposal lets the building component keep
        working—as memory.
      </p>
      <div class="method-architecture">
        <article>
          <small>I · Humanistic elevation</small>
          <h3>People on the building</h3>
          <p>
            Project the building's people onto the fair-faced concrete, giving the cold architecture
            the life of those who use it.
          </p>
          <b>Building + people</b>
        </article>
        <article>
          <small>II · Memory retention</small>
          <h3>A component that stays</h3>
          <p>
            The roof accompanied the school for fourteen years; the memory can keep working in a new
            role.
          </p>
          <b>Keep the remnant</b>
        </article>
        <article>
          <small>III · Spatial extension</small>
          <h3>The atrium as garden</h3>
          <p>
            The roof's own shape bridges the lower outdoor platform and the upper roof garden,
            breaking the flat eye-level view of the first floor.
          </p>
          <b>Garden, extended</b>
        </article>
      </div>
      <p class="portfolio-caption">
        The roof was later cleared away; this document remains a proposal for what could have been.
      </p>
    </div>
  </PortfolioSection>

  <PortfolioSection>
    <PortfolioSectionHeader title="Spatial Analysis" />
    <p class="portfolio-copy portfolio-copy--flush">
      The installation sits in the atrium south of the college library. The audience walks and
      interacts along the first-floor corridor; the camera and projector face the roof remnant in
      the atrium below.
    </p>
    <ProjectFigure class="project-figure--white">
      <ResponsiveImage
        src="/pictures/work/rooftop-garden/rg-p4-1.png"
        alt="Spatial analysis: installation position and viewing position in Weixin Hall"
      />
      <figcaption>Installation & viewing positions.</figcaption>
    </ProjectFigure>
  </PortfolioSection>

  <PortfolioSection class="portfolio-section--dark">
    <div class="portfolio-section__inner">
      <PortfolioSectionHeader title="The Interaction: A Flower Shadow" />
      <p class="portfolio-copy portfolio-copy--flush">
        A camera watches the first-floor corridor. When a passerby walks through the interaction
        area, the computer extracts their silhouette in real time and projects a flower-composed
        human shadow onto the fallen roof in the atrium.
      </p>
      <ProjectFigure>
        <ResponsiveImage
          src="/pictures/work/rooftop-garden/rg-p5-1.png"
          alt="Interaction diagram: camera on the corridor, projection on the roof remnant"
        />
        <figcaption>Interaction diagram · flower shadow rendered with Grok.</figcaption>
      </ProjectFigure>
      <p class="portfolio-copy portfolio-copy--flush">
        The person appears inside the roof remnant, as if they had become part of the garden the
        roof once covered. The most beautiful flowers in the building meet the most spirited people
        in the building.
      </p>
      <p class="portfolio-copy portfolio-copy--flush">
        When projection is not possible during the day, the roof remains in the atrium as an
        abstract sculpture.
      </p>
    </div>
  </PortfolioSection>

  <PortfolioSection>
    <PortfolioSectionHeader title="Technical Notes" />
    <div class="method-architecture method-architecture--light">
      <article>
        <small>01</small>
        <h3>Non-planar projection</h3>
        <p>
          The remnant is not a flat screen: content must be mapped to its shape, and the surface
          treated so it displays well.
        </p>
        <b>Projection mapping</b>
      </article>
      <article>
        <small>02</small>
        <h3>Silhouette extraction</h3>
        <p>The camera's live image is processed to extract the passerby's overall outline.</p>
        <b>Computer vision</b>
      </article>
      <article>
        <small>03</small>
        <h3>Flower-image generation</h3>
        <p>The extracted outline is turned into a moving figure composed of flowers.</p>
        <b>Image generation</b>
      </article>
    </div>
  </PortfolioSection>

  <PortfolioSection>
    <PortfolioSectionHeader title="References" />
    <p class="portfolio-copy portfolio-copy--flush">
      The design grew from a photograph taken on 20 September 2024, the day the roof fell, and from
      the architectural documentation and on-site survey of Weixin Hall.
    </p>
    <FigureRow class="figure-row--two">
      <figure>
        <ResponsiveImage
          src="/pictures/work/rooftop-garden/rg-p8-1.png"
          alt="The author's photograph of the fallen roof"
        />
        <figcaption>The author's photograph, 20 September 2024 · design inspiration.</figcaption>
      </figure>
      <figure class="figure--match-first">
        <ResponsiveImage
          src="/pictures/work/rooftop-garden/rg-p9-1.png"
          alt="Architectural documentation of Weixin Hall"
        />
        <figcaption>Architectural documentation of Weixin Hall.</figcaption>
      </figure>
    </FigureRow>
  </PortfolioSection>

  <PortfolioSection class="portfolio-section--closing">
    <div class="portfolio-section__label">What this is</div>
    <p class="portfolio-statement">
      A proposal that keeps an accident as memory—rather than erasing it—by making the building's
      people visible on the very piece the storm moved.
    </p>
  </PortfolioSection>
</ProjectLayout>
